    "description": "What an orangecheck origin is: an auth host's issuer (where its keys, revoke and the native trade are) and where me.ochk.io is (the sign-in rule and the list of ended sessions). Read by auth-core (src/__tests__/issuer-origin.test.ts) and sdk-py (tests/test_issuer_origin.py), so every caller in both languages accepts the same set and names the same origin. The rule: printable ASCII with no space; lower-cased; one trailing slash dropped; then exactly scheme://host or scheme://host:port. The scheme is https, or http when the host is localhost, 127.0.0.1 or [::1]. The host is [::1], four dotted decimal octets (0 to 255, no leading zero), or dot-separated labels of letters, digits and inner hyphens (1 to 63 characters) whose last label starts with a letter. The port is 1 to 65535 with no leading zero, and never the scheme's own (443, 80). accepted maps each input to the origin a caller uses; everything in refused and refused_values is refused.",
